# Bicep Authoring Standards

Before authoring any resource, confirm it falls within the allowed capability categories in `docs/scope.md`. Do not author Entra/Graph resources, RBAC, management groups, subscription vending, or general infrastructure (networking, VMs, storage, databases, Key Vault, app hosting) — these are prohibited or require an explicit architecture decision.

## Module selection

- Consider Azure Verified Modules (AVM) — https://azure.github.io/Azure-Verified-Modules/ — and use one where it genuinely reduces implementation or maintenance effort. Do not reach for AVM (or any external module) simply because a module exists; a plain resource declaration is often simpler and easier to maintain for small, focused compositions.
- Pin exact versions for any external module reference (AVM, Bicep Registry, or other) — never float an unpinned or `:latest` version.
- Verify resource types, properties, and API versions against authoritative Microsoft documentation (https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/) or the ARM resource schema before writing a resource block.
- Bicep MCP tools (e.g. `get_azure_resource_type_schema`, `get_bicep_best_practices`, `format_bicep_file`) are useful when available, but their availability is not a prerequisite for development — Microsoft Learn and the Bicep CLI are sufficient on their own.

## Naming

Follow the naming convention required by the target customer, subscription, or resource type. Do not force a fixed pattern such as `{type}-{workload}-{environment}-{region}` onto scopes or resources where workload, environment, or region genuinely do not apply (e.g. management-group-scoped resources or cross-project shared services).

## targetScope rules

- `subscription` — resource groups, subscription-level role assignments (only where explicitly approved), policies
- `resourceGroup` — any resource that lives inside a resource group
- Never default every module to `subscription`. Choose based on the resource being deployed.

## Parameter contracts

Include only the parameters a composition or module actually uses. Do not require every parameter file to declare `environment`, `location`, `purpose`, and `tags` by default — add each one only when the deployment genuinely needs it.

Do not hardcode or assume a specific workload (e.g. `purpose = 'example'`) in reusable modules or shared compositions. One workload is the primary but not the only consumer of this repository's reusable capabilities — workload/project identifiers are supplied by the caller through `config/`, so the same composition stays usable for other projects, subscriptions, or management-group scopes.
